This is a classic Enterprise SAST (Static Application Security Testing) report. You do not need to change your entire backend system design!
What JFrog/Fortify is doing is called Taint Analysis. It tracks a variable (like batchId) from the moment it enters your Controller (the "Source") all the way down to where it is used (the "Sink"). If it sees that the variable is used unsafely anywhere in that journey, it flags the Controller line.
Here is the exact breakdown of why these 4 errors happened and exactly what to replace to pass the security gate.
1. SQL Injection (Critical)
The Problem: The scanner points to VoucherQueryController.java, but the actual vulnerability is inside your VoucherQueryService.java. In methods like generateBatchExcel, getRequestCountByBatchId, and getVidDetails, you are using string concatenation (e.g., "... WHERE BATCH_ID = '" + safeBatchId + "'"). Even if you sanitized it, SAST tools will always flag string concatenation in SQL as a Critical SQL Injection.
The Fix: Use parameterized queries (?) for those specific methods in VoucherQueryService.java.
Open VoucherQueryService.java and replace these 3 methods:
A. Replace getRequestCountByBatchId:
    public long getRequestCountByBatchId(String batchId) {
        log.info("COUNT_QUERY_START | BatchID: {}", SecuritySanitizer.sanitizeForLog(batchId));
        String safeBatchId = SecuritySanitizer.sanitizeId(batchId);

        // FIX: Use '?' instead of String concatenation
        String sql = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM REQUEST_VOUCHER WHERE batch_id = ?";
        Long count = jdbcTemplate.queryForObject(sql, Long.class, safeBatchId);
        return count != null ? count : 0L;
    }

