2. Path Manipulation (Critical)
The Problem: In VoucherBulkUploadController.java, you used new File(originalFilename).getName(). SAST tools flag new File(...) when dealing with user uploads because it touches the Operating System's file API with untrusted data, risking Directory Traversal attacks (e.g., ../../../etc/passwd).
The Fix: Use Spring's safe string-based path cleaner instead.
Open VoucherBulkUploadController.java and replace lines 35-37:
REPLACE THIS:
        // FORTIFY FIX: Often Misused: File Upload (Strips path traversal sequences)
        String originalFilename = file.getOriginalFilename() != null ? file.getOriginalFilename() : "unknown.xlsx";
        String safeFilename = new File(originalFilename).getName();

WITH THIS:
        // FORTIFY FIX: Path Manipulation - Use Spring's string-based cleaner instead of OS-level File APIs
        String originalFilename = file.getOriginalFilename() != null ? file.getOriginalFilename() : "unknown.xlsx";
        String safeFilename = org.springframework.util.StringUtils.cleanPath(originalFilename);

3. Dynamic Code Evaluation (Critical)
The Problem: In CacheConfig.java, you used .allowIfBaseType(Object.class). JFrog flags this because allowing Object.class defeats the purpose of the Polymorphic Validator. If any object is allowed as a base, attackers can still inject malicious payloads for Remote Code Execution (RCE) during Redis deserialization.
The Fix: Remove the overly broad Object.class rule and restrict the base types.
Open CacheConfig.java and replace the PolymorphicTypeValidator block (around lines 23-31):
REPLACE THIS:
        PolymorphicTypeValidator ptv = BasicPolymorphicTypeValidator.builder()
                .allowIfBaseType(Object.class)
                .allowIfSubType("com.fincore.VoucherService.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.util.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.math.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.time.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.lang.")
                .build();

WITH THIS:
        // FORTIFY FIX: Dynamic Code Evaluation - Removed broad Object.class and restricted to strict subtypes
        PolymorphicTypeValidator ptv = BasicPolymorphicTypeValidator.builder()
                .allowIfSubType("com.fincore.VoucherService.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.util.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.math.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.time.")
                .allowIfSubType("java.lang.String")
                .allowIfSubType("java.lang.Number")
                .allowIfSubType("java.lang.Boolean")
                .build();

4. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) (High)
The Problem: In VoucherActionController.java, you are returning e.getMessage() directly into your JSON API response (e.g., new ApiResponseDto("Error", e.getMessage(), null)). If the exception message contains user input (like a malicious batch ID: <script>alert(1)</script>), and a browser renders it, it causes Reflected XSS.
The Fix: Sanitize the exception message by stripping out HTML characters before sending it back in the JSON body.
Open VoucherActionController.java and replace the catch blocks in deletePendingBatch (around lines 100-108):
REPLACE THIS:
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN).body(new ApiResponseDto("Error", e.getMessage(), null));
        } catch (CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(new ApiResponseDto("Error", e.getMessage(), null));
